## The problem

Revenue, engagement and growth numbers lived in several different places, and the team
assembled them by hand to see how a week had gone. That report was already stale by the
time it was finished, and nobody wanted to build it twice in a day.

## The pipeline

Scheduled Python jobs collect from each source, normalise what comes back, and write it
to a store the dashboard reads. Collection and presentation are deliberately separate.
Sources rate limit, go down, and change their response shape without warning, and none
of that should take the dashboard down with it. When a source fails, the last known
good numbers stay on screen and the failure reports itself rather than rendering an
empty chart that looks like a business problem.

Timezones caused more bugs here than anything else. Different sources report in
different zones, and a day boundary interpreted in the wrong one quietly shifts every
daily figure. Storing everything in UTC and converting once, at the edge, settled it.

## Alerts people don't learn to ignore

Alerts go out through Telegram and Discord, because that is where the team already
talks. The real failure mode of an alerting system is being ignored, so thresholds fire
on things actually worth interrupting someone for, and a condition that is still true
does not notify again. An alert that arrives every hour becomes furniture.

## Stack

Python on AWS with scheduled jobs, delivering through the Telegram and Discord APIs.
